Ladybird now fires a mouse button’s click, auxclick, and contextmenu
events as PointerEvents, and so do the other engines: Blink creates them
in MouseEventManager::DispatchMouseEvent(), and WebKit in its Element::
dispatchMouseEvent(). Gecko sends them as WidgetPointerEvents, with the
messages ePointerClick and ePointerAuxClick, which EventDispatcher::
CreateEvent() turns into DOM PointerEvents.

Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5, all three
fire all three events as PointerEvents with pointerType mouse. Chrome
leaves isPrimary false on them, as the spec requires and as Ladybird
does; Firefox and WebKit set it. A contextmenu’s pressure is 0 in
Chrome and Firefox, as in Ladybird, and 0.5 in WebKit. Its detail is 0
in Chrome and WebKit, as in Ladybird, and 1 in Firefox. And on an
embedded YouTube player, a click on the progress bar seeks, and a click
on the video pauses and resumes it, in Chrome and in Ladybird with this
change — but not in Ladybird without it.
